299:   The DHT routes only registered :segment/<algorithm>-... content addresses\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc:300:   and records no source identity: there are no roots, CAS records, deletes,\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc:301:   or intake streams.\"\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc:306:  (when-not (fn? (:put-bytes-fn local))\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc:307:    (throw (ex-info \"dao.jing.dht local requires :put-bytes-fn\"\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc:312:  (let [closed-atom (atom false)\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc:313:        handle {:net net, :local local, :closed-atom closed-atom}]\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc:315:           :put-bytes-fn (make-put handle)\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc:317:           :close-fn (make-close handle))))\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:2:description: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:14:### Foundational Axioms\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:16:1. **Everything is a Stream**: All IO and data flow through append-only streams.\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:22:4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:24:## Non-Negotiable Invariants\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:28:- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:37:Source scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:41:## Streams\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:43:All IO is modeled as a stream. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:44:No direct function-to-function coupling without a stream boundary.\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:45:Side effects must appear as stream emissions.\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:46:Streams are values that can be sent through streams.\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:47:Streams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:49:A stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:52:stream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:53:the very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:57:and the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:58:becomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:59:the stream's own.\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:62:Agents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Agent behavior is specified entirely through stream effects.\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:64:See [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:65:See [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:69:A host boundary is a stream boundary, never a function call. The one\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:70:sanctioned function-shaped contact is a `dao.stream` handle operation, whose\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:71:exhaustive outcome map is stream protocol, not an application callback.\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:74:happen. A host interpreter consumes that stream, performs the operation, and\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:80:**Events in.** When the host initiates (a socket receives, a connection drops,\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:82:nothing. An interpreter reads that stream and decides what the event means.\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:83:Constructively: **a callback is replaced by a function that transforms an event\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:84:to data on a stream.** The host's demand for a callback stops at that function.\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:93:may close that resource to make the boundary observably gone; this is teardown,\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:94:not an upward callback.\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:97:place holding a host error object, and no host type crosses onto the stream. A\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:99:additive evolution; that envelope remains plain data and creates no separate\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:103:Which stream an adapter writes to is the caller's composition, never the\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:105:transport's private stream or into a medium many interpreters read. This keeps\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:107:constructs the adapter supplies the stream.\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:111:happen below the transform: malformed wire input is transport machinery, not an\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:113:(axiom 2): an event that is noise to one interpreter is signal to another that\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:121:callback, not removed it, even when every value it passes is plain data; if\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:122:that callback has a meaningful return value, it is a synchronous call in both\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:128:- **Macros are stream topology**: an evaluator knows nothing about macro expansion; expansion happens on the syntax side of a medium boundary, upstream of any evaluator.\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:129:- **Peer observers, one stream**: a generic evaluator and `dao.space` independently observe the same stream topic, as described under Streams. This is not a claim about any two consumers regardless of what they consume.\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:130:- **Observers are independent and optional**: nothing requires a medium to have any particular set of consumers. Which observers exist for a stream is a per-composition choice, not a design-time commitment.\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:135:Stigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:137:**Systems emerge from interpreters of pure data.** Streams are the\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:142:  ordinary streams.\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:152:canonical content-hashed structure (an AST grammar, a datom schema, a row\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:154:`dao.space.query/q` can already compute the fact. If it can, it belongs as a\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:155:query or projection: new persisted structure migrates every downstream\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:161:See [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../agents/malleability.md) for complete design guidance (cohesion, coupling, bounded complexity, evolution, testing strategy).\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:170:- Name things after what they do to streams.\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:174:- Do not invent abstractions that hide streams.\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:175:- Do not suggest mainstream frameworks unless explicitly asked.\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:179:- Program store writes go through `engine/store-put`; any other store write\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:187:1. Decompose it into streams.\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:188:2. Identify invariants.\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc:1:(ns dao.stream.udp\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc:2:  \"The UDP channel of dao.stream.remote.md (3.2): the WebSocket deposit\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc:6:   host adapter feeds every received datagram to `receive!` with its\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc:7:   raw bytes and the source address the socket saw. One CBOR value,\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc:9:   fragment envelope included -- the figure `dao.jing.dht.node` already\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc:11:   is split into fragment envelopes, channel-internal: neither the\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc:12:   mirror step nor the link (dao.stream.remote) ever sees one, only the\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc:13:   complete decoded message `receive!` reassembles and deposits.\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc:17:   the remote host:port it saw, so this port's own reassembly key\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc:19:   fragment's own carried id is unique only per asking side, so the\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc:20:   direction distinguishes a fragment split from an outgoing request\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc:24:   `:dao.stream.udp/max-message-bytes` (default 64 KiB) refuses an\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc:26:   and `:dao.stream.udp/max-partial-messages`, evicting the oldest\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc:30:   deposited as one event, `{:dao.stream.udp/attachment id\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc:31:   :dao.stream.udp/source {:dao.stream.udp/host h\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc:32:   :dao.stream.udp/port p} :dao.stream.udp/value v}`, onto the shared\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc:34:   attachments, one per remote address, so this medium is shared the\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc:42:   whose `append!` sends toward the descriptor's address, fragmenting\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc:47:   claim, because `receive!` is the only place a source address is\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc:50:  (:require [dao.stream :as stream]\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc:51:            [dao.stream.cbor :as cbor])\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc:55:(def transport-type :dao.stream/udp)\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc:59:  \"The whole encoded datagram's budget, fragment envelope included: the\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc:61:   MTUs (docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md 3.2).\"\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc:65:(def default-max-message-bytes\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc:66:  \"64 KiB, the default reassembly maximum (3.2).\"\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc:70:(def default-max-partial-messages\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc:71:  \"No default is named in 3.2; this is this implementation's own bound\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc:78:  \"{:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/udp :dao.stream/identity id\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc:79:     :dao.stream.udp/host h :dao.stream.udp/port p} (3.2).\"\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc:82:       (= transport-type (:dao.