{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"agent_message","text":"Two contract gaps stand out so far: the proposed memory-log outbox cannot support a durable acknowledgement, and the five slices do not deliver root discovery. I’m checking the transport and store contracts before fixing the dependency order."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc | sed -n '1,317p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/jing/dht/node.cljc | sed -n '65,345p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/jing/dht/kad.cljc | sed -n '1,68p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc | sed -n '1,317p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/jing/dht/node.cljc | sed -n '65,345p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/jing/dht/kad.cljc | sed -n '1,68p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t(ns dao.jing.dht\n     2\t  \"DHT content-store backend for the dao.jing storage boundary\n     3\t   (docs/design/dao.jing.md).\n     4\t\n     5\t   create-content-dht wraps an IDhtNet transport and a local content handle\n     6\t   (e.g. dao.jing.mem/create-content-mem) into a content-store handle map:\n     7\t\n     8\t     {:net net, :local local, :closed-atom a,\n     9\t      :put-bytes-fn f, :get-bytes-fn g, :close-fn c}\n    10\t\n    11\t   consumed by dao.jing/materialize!, dao.jing/get, and dao.jing/close!.\n    12\t\n    13\t   Put validates the address-payload pair, writes the local backend, then\n    14\t   best-effort replicates to the k nearest non-self peers. Get reads the\n    15\t   local backend first and falls back to the grid, verifying every fetched\n    16\t   payload against its requested content address and caching verified values\n    17\t   through the local backend.\n    18\t\n    19\t   There are no roots, no CAS records, no deletes, and no intake streams\n    20\t   here: the DHT routes segment content addresses (:segment/<algo>-...),\n    21\t   derives its routing target solely from the content digest, and never\n    22\t   records which stream or peer carried a payload.\"\n    23\t  (:require [dao.jing :as jing]\n    24\t            [dao.jing.cbor :as cbor]\n    25\t            [dao.jing.dht.kad :as kad]))\n    26\t\n    27\t\n    28\t;; =============================================================================\n    29\t;; Routing identity\n    30\t;; =============================================================================\n    31\t\n    32\t(defn node-id\n    33\t  \"Deterministic node id: SHA-256 of host:port.\"\n    34\t  [host port]\n    35\t  (jing/sha256 (str host \":\" port)))\n    36\t\n    37\t\n    38\t(defn- content-target\n    39\t  \"The routing target for a strict segment content address: its\n    40\t   content digest. Non-segment addresses are outside the DHT and throw; there\n    41\t   is no root class, so nothing is ever hashed by key name.\"\n    42\t  [address]\n    43\t  (when-not (jing/segment-address? address)\n    44\t    (throw (ex-info \"dao.jing.dht: not a segment content address\"\n    45\t                    {:address address})))\n    46\t  (jing/segment-hash address))\n    47\t\n    48\t\n    49\t;; =============================================================================\n    50\t;; Transport boundary\n    51\t;; =============================================================================\n    52\t\n    53\t(defprotocol IDhtNet\n    54\t  \"The per-peer RPC surface dao.jing.dht requires of a transport.\"\n    55\t\n    56\t  (self-peer\n    57\t    [net]\n    58\t    \"This node's own {:id :host :port} peer map.\")\n    59\t\n    60\t  (known-peers\n    61\t    [net target-id n]\n    62\t    \"The n locally-known peers nearest target-id, nearest first. No IO.\")\n    63\t\n    64\t  (find-closer\n    65\t    [net peer target-id]\n    66\t    \"Ask peer for the peers it knows nearest target-id.\n    67\t     Returns a seq of peer maps, or nil when unreachable.\")\n    68\t\n    69\t  (store-content!\n    70\t    [net peer address payload]\n    71\t    \"Ask peer to hold content address under payload. Best effort; returns a\n    72\t     boolean acknowledgement (false or nil when the peer is unreachable or\n    73\t     refuses).\n    74\t     Implementations must own bounded transport timeouts and return false/nil\n    75\t     when unreachable.\")\n    76\t\n    77\t  (fetch-content\n    78\t    [net peer address]\n    79\t    \"Ask peer for the content stored at address. Returns\n    80\t     {:found? bool :value v} when reachable, or nil when unreachable.\n    81\t     Implementations must own bounded transport timeouts and return nil\n    82\t     when unreachable.\")\n    83\t\n    84\t  (close-net!\n    85\t    [net]\n    86\t    \"Release the transport's local resources (socket, threads).\"))\n    87\t\n    88\t\n    89\t(def ^:private content-missing\n    90\t  \"Internal not-found sentinel for local read-backs, never exposed. An\n    91\t   opaque per-host identity object, never a keyword: a keyword sentinel\n    92\t   could be confused with a genuinely stored payload.\"\n    93\t  #?(:cljd (Object.)\n    94\t     :clj (Object.)\n    95\t     :cljs (js-obj)))\n    96\t\n    97\t\n    98\t;; =============================================================================\n    99\t;; Iterative lookup\n   100\t;; =============================================================================\n   101\t\n   102\t(def ^:private alpha\n   103\t  \"Lookup concurrency width.\"\n   104\t  3)\n   105\t\n   106\t\n   107\t(defn lookup\n   108\t  \"Iteratively converge on the kad/k known non-self peers nearest target-id. Queried\n   109\t   peer ids are deduplicated, so a peer is never asked twice in the same\n   110\t   lookup even when different routes return it with different metadata.\"\n   111\t  [net target-id]\n   112\t  (let [self (self-peer net)]\n   113\t    (loop [discovered (into {}\n   114\t                            (comp (remove #(= (:id self) (:id %)))\n   115\t                                  (map (juxt :id identity)))\n   116\t                            (known-peers net target-id kad/k))\n   117\t           queried-ids #{(:id self)}]\n   118\t      (let [closest-k (->> (vals discovered)\n   119\t                           (sort-by #(kad/distance (:id %) target-id))\n   120\t                           (take kad/k))\n   121\t            candidates (->> closest-k\n   122\t                            (remove #(contains? queried-ids (:id %)))\n   123\t                            (take alpha))]\n   124\t        (if (empty? candidates)\n   125\t          (vec closest-k)\n   126\t          (recur (into discovered\n   127\t                       (comp (mapcat #(find-closer net % target-id))\n   128\t                             (remove #(= (:id self) (:id %)))\n   129\t                             (map (juxt :id identity)))\n   130\t                       candidates)\n   131\t                 (into queried-ids (map :id) candidates)))))))\n   132\t\n   133\t\n   134\t;; =============================================================================\n   135\t;; Content-store effects\n   136\t;; =============================================================================\n   137\t\n   138\t#_{:clj-kondo/ignore [:unused-binding]}\n   139\t\n   140\t\n   141\t(defn- with-lock\n   142\t  \"Run f under lock on hosts with a monitor primitive; direct call elsewhere.\"\n   143\t  [lock f]\n   144\t  #?(:clj (locking lock (f))\n   145\t     :default (f)))\n   146\t\n   147\t\n   148\t(defn- ensure-open\n   149\t  [{:keys [closed-atom]}]\n   150\t  (when @closed-atom\n   151\t    (throw (ex-info \"dao.jing.dht: DHT content store is closed\"\n   152\t                    {:closed true}))))\n   153\t\n   154\t\n   155\t(defn- validate-address-bytes!\n   156\t  \"Reject a put before any local or network action: the address must be a\n   157\t   valid segment content address whose digest is the hash of the bytes.\"\n   158\t  [address bs]\n   159\t  (when-not (jing/segment-address? address)\n   160\t    (throw (ex-info \"dao.jing.dht: not a segment content address\"\n   161\t                    {:address address})))\n   162\t  (when-not (jing/segment-bytes-match? address bs)\n   163\t    (throw (ex-info \"dao.jing.dht: content address does not match the bytes\"\n   164\t                    {:address address}))))\n   165\t\n   166\t\n   167\t(defn- accept-bytes\n   168\t  \"The canonical bytes of the Base64 text a peer answered for address, or\n   169\t   nil when the text, the digest, or the payload's canonicality fails: a\n   170\t   peer is untrusted, so this is the one ingress check before caching.\"\n   171\t  [address b64]\n   172\t  (try (let [bs (jing/base64->bytes b64)]\n   173\t         (when (jing/segment-bytes-match? address bs)\n   174\t           (cbor/decode bs)\n   175\t           bs))\n   176\t       (catch #?(:clj Throwable\n   177\t                 :cljs :default\n   178\t                 :cljd Object)\n   179\t              _\n   180\t         nil)))\n   181\t\n   182\t\n   183\t(defn- safe-store-content!\n   184\t  [net peer address payload]\n   185\t  (try (store-content! net peer address payload)\n   186\t       (catch #?(:clj Throwable\n   187\t                 :cljs :default\n   188\t                 :cljd Object)\n   189\t              _\n   190\t         false)))\n   191\t\n   192\t\n   193\t(defn- safe-fetch-content\n   194\t  [net peer address]\n   195\t  (try (fetch-content net peer address)\n   196\t       (catch #?(:clj Throwable\n   197\t                 :cljs :default\n   198\t                 :cljd Object)\n   199\t              _\n   200\t         nil)))\n   201\t\n   202\t\n   203\t(defn- make-put\n   204\t  [{:keys [net local], :as handle}]\n   205\t  (fn [address bs]\n   206\t    (validate-address-bytes! address bs)\n   207\t    (ensure-open handle)\n   208\t    (let [result ((:put-bytes-fn local) address bs)]\n   209\t      (when-not (#{:inserted :present} result)\n   210\t        (throw (ex-info \"dao.jing.dht: invalid local put result\"\n   211\t                        {:result result, :address address})))\n   212\t      (let [self-id (:id (self-peer net))\n   213\t            b64 (jing/bytes->base64 bs)\n   214\t            peers (remove #(= self-id (:id %))\n   215\t                          (lookup net (content-target address)))]\n   216\t        #?(:clj (run! deref\n   217\t                      (mapv (fn [peer]\n   218\t                              (future\n   219\t                                (safe-store-content! net peer address b64)))\n   220\t                            peers))\n   221\t           :default (run! (fn [peer]\n   222\t                            (safe-store-content! net peer address b64))\n   223\t                          peers)))\n   224\t      result)))\n   225\t\n   226\t\n   227\t(defn- make-get\n   228\t  [{:keys [net local], :as handle}]\n   229\t  (fn [address not-found]\n   230\t    (ensure-open handle)\n   231\t    (let [v ((:get-bytes-fn local) address content-missing)]\n   232\t      (if (not (identical? v content-missing))\n   233\t        v\n   234\t        (let [self-id (:id (self-peer net))\n   235\t              fetched\n   236\t              (some (fn [peer]\n   237\t                      (when (not= self-id (:id peer))\n   238\t                        (when-let [res (safe-fetch-content net peer address)]\n   239\t                          (when (:found? res)\n   240\t                            (when-let [bs (accept-bytes address (:value res))]\n   241\t                              [bs])))))\n   242\t                    (lookup net (content-target address)))]\n   243\t          (if fetched\n   244\t            (let [bs (first fetched)\n   245\t                  result ((:put-bytes-fn local) address bs)]\n   246\t              (when-not (#{:inserted :present} result)\n   247\t                (throw\n   248\t                  (ex-info\n   249\t                    \"dao.jing.dht: fetched content could not be cached\"\n   250\t                    {:address address, :result result})))\n   251\t              bs)\n   252\t            not-found))))))\n   253\t\n   254\t\n   255\t(defn- make-close\n   256\t  [{:keys [net local closed-atom]}]\n   257\t  (fn []\n   258\t    (with-lock closed-atom\n   259\t      (fn []\n   260\t        (when-not @closed-atom\n   261\t          (let [err-net (try (close-net! net)\n   262\t                             nil\n   263\t                             (catch #?(:clj Throwable\n   264\t                                       :cljs :default\n   265\t                                       :cljd Object)\n   266\t                                    e\n   267\t                               e))\n   268\t                err-local (try (jing/close! local)\n   269\t                               nil\n   270\t                               (catch #?(:clj Throwable\n   271\t                                         :cljs :default\n   272\t                                         :cljd Object)\n   273\t                                      e\n   274\t                                 e))]\n   275\t            (if (or err-net err-local)\n   276\t              (throw (or err-net err-local))\n   277\t              (reset! closed-atom true))))\n   278\t        nil))))\n   279\t\n   280\t\n   281\t;; =============================================================================\n   282\t;; Handle constructor\n   283\t;; =============================================================================\n   284\t\n   285\t(defn create-content-dht\n   286\t  \"Wrap an IDhtNet transport and a local content handle as a DHT\n   287\t   content-store handle.\n   288\t\n   289\t   The returned handle is plain data:\n   290\t     {:net net, :local local, :closed-atom a,\n   291\t      :put-bytes-fn f, :get-bytes-fn g, :close-fn c}\n   292\t\n   293\t   and works with dao.jing/materialize!, dao.jing/get, and dao.jing/close!.\n   294\t   :net is an IDhtNet transport; :local is a content handle carrying\n   295\t   :put-bytes-fn and :get-bytes-fn (dao.jing.mem/create-content-mem or\n   296\t   equivalent); :closed-atom is the store's explicit private state and close\n   297\t   lock.\n   298\t\n   299\t   The DHT routes only registered :segment/<algorithm>-... content addresses\n   300\t   and records no source identity: there are no roots, CAS records, deletes,\n   301\t   or intake streams.\"\n   302\t  [{:keys [net local]}]\n   303\t  (when-not (and net local)\n   304\t    (throw (ex-info \"dao.jing.dht requires :net and :local\"\n   305\t                    {:net net, :local local})))\n   306\t  (when-not (fn? (:put-bytes-fn local))\n   307\t    (throw (ex-info \"dao.jing.dht local requires :put-bytes-fn\"\n   308\t                    {:local local})))\n   309\t  (when-not (fn? (:get-bytes-fn local))\n   310\t    (throw (ex-info \"dao.jing.dht local requires :get-bytes-fn\"\n   311\t                    {:local local})))\n   312\t  (let [closed-atom (atom false)\n   313\t        handle {:net net, :local local, :closed-atom closed-atom}]\n   314\t    (assoc handle\n   315\t           :put-bytes-fn (make-put handle)\n   316\t           :get-bytes-fn (make-get handle)\n   317\t           :close-fn (make-close handle))))\n    65\t          [^DatagramPacket packet]\n    66\t          (transit/decode (String. (.getData packet)\n    67\t                                   (.getOffset packet)\n    68\t                                   (.getLength packet)\n    69\t                                   \"UTF-8\"))))\n    70\t\n    71\t\n    72\t#?(:cljd nil\n    73\t   :clj\n    74\t   (defn- send-datagram!\n    75\t     [^DatagramSocket socket ^bytes payload ^InetAddress addr port]\n    76\t     (when (> (alength payload) max-datagram)\n    77\t       (throw\n    78\t         (ex-info\n    79\t           \"payload exceeds the datagram budget; DRDS fragmentation is not implemented\"\n    80\t           {:size (alength payload), :max max-datagram})))\n    81\t     (.send socket\n    82\t            (DatagramPacket. payload (alength payload) addr (int port)))))\n    83\t\n    84\t\n    85\t;; =============================================================================\n    86\t;; Request handling (the server half)\n    87\t;; =============================================================================\n    88\t\n    89\t#?(:cljd nil\n    90\t   :clj\n    91\t   (def ^:private missing\n    92\t     \"Internal not-found sentinel for served :fetch-content reads, never\n    93\t      exposed. An opaque per-host identity object, never a keyword: a\n    94\t      keyword sentinel could be confused with a genuinely stored payload,\n    95\t      and nil is a legal one.\"\n    96\t     (Object.)))\n    97\t\n    98\t\n    99\t#?(:cljd nil\n   100\t   :clj\n   101\t   (defn- handle\n   102\t     \"Serve one request against this node's local content store.\"\n   103\t     [local table msg]\n   104\t     (case (:op msg)\n   105\t       :ping {:pong true}\n   106\t       :find-node {:peers (kad/nearest @table (:target msg) kad/k)}\n   107\t       ;; peers are untrusted: the address must be the exact strict\n   108\t       ;; segment address for the payload, never a bare hash, a\n   109\t       ;; foreign namespace, or a mismatched value, and the local\n   110\t       ;; backend's verdict must be an explicit :inserted/:present\n   111\t       ;; :v is the canonical payload bytes as padded standard Base64;\n   112\t       ;; the bytes must decode as one canonical payload (the ingress\n   113\t       ;; check) before the local byte store sees them\n   114\t       :store-content\n   115\t       (let [{:keys [address v]} msg]\n   116\t         (try\n   117\t           (if (jing/segment-address? address)\n   118\t             (let [bs (jing/base64->bytes v)]\n   119\t               (if (jing/segment-bytes-match? address bs)\n   120\t                 (do (cbor/decode bs)\n   121\t                     (let [result ((:put-bytes-fn local) address bs)]\n   122\t                       (if (#{:inserted :present} result)\n   123\t                         {:ok true}\n   124\t                         {:ok false})))\n   125\t                 {:ok false}))\n   126\t             {:ok false})\n   127\t           (catch Exception _ {:ok false})))\n   128\t       ;; :found travels explicitly on both reads: nil is a legal stored\n   129\t       ;; value, so presence cannot be inferred from :v\n   130\t       :fetch-content\n   131\t       (let [bs (if (jing/segment-address? (:address msg))\n   132\t                  ((:get-bytes-fn local) (:address msg) missing)\n   133\t                  missing)]\n   134\t         (if (identical? bs missing)\n   135\t           {:found false}\n   136\t           {:found true, :v (jing/bytes->base64 bs)}))\n   137\t       ;; no root or CAS op exists on this wire; unknown ops are\n   138\t       ;; thrown and answered as {:error ...} by the receiver\n   139\t       (throw (ex-info \"dao.jing.dht.node: unknown op\" {:op (:op msg)})))))\n   140\t\n   141\t\n   142\t#?(:cljd nil\n   143\t   :clj\n   144\t   (defn- start-receiver!\n   145\t     \"One daemon thread per node: decodes datagrams, observes senders into\n   146\t      the routing table, delivers replies to pending requests, and serves\n   147\t      requests. Replies go to the datagram's source address, not the claimed\n   148\t      :from.\"\n   149\t     [^DatagramSocket socket local table self ^ConcurrentHashMap pending]\n   150\t     (doto\n   151\t       (Thread.\n   152\t         (fn []\n   153\t           (let [buf (byte-array 65536)\n   154\t                 packet (DatagramPacket. buf (alength buf))]\n   155\t             (while (not (.isClosed socket))\n   156\t               ;; three failure domains, contained separately so no\n   157\t               ;; single datagram or transient fault can kill the\n   158\t               ;; loop: the loop exits only when the socket closes\n   159\t               (when (try (.receive socket packet)\n   160\t                          true\n   161\t                          (catch Exception _\n   162\t                            ;; socket closed -> while exits; any\n   163\t                            ;; other receive fault: back off instead\n   164\t                            ;; of spinning hot. 10ms is not tuned,\n   165\t                            ;; only bounded: short enough to shrug\n   166\t                            ;; off a transient fault, long enough to\n   167\t                            ;; cap a persistent one at ~100\n   168\t                            ;; attempts/sec\n   169\t                            (when-not (.isClosed socket) (Thread/sleep 10))\n   170\t                            false))\n   171\t                 (let [msg (try (decode packet) (catch Exception _ nil))]\n   172\t                   (when (map? msg)\n   173\t                     (try (when-let [from (:from msg)]\n   174\t                            (when (not= (:id from) (:id self))\n   175\t                              (swap! table kad/observe (:id self) from)))\n   176\t                          (if (= :reply (:op msg))\n   177\t                            (when-let [p (.remove pending (:rpc msg))]\n   178\t                              (deliver p (:value msg)))\n   179\t                            (let [value (try (handle local table msg)\n   180\t                                             (catch Exception e\n   181\t                                               {:error (str (type e))}))\n   182\t                                  reply (encode {:op :reply,\n   183\t                                                 :rpc (:rpc msg),\n   184\t                                                 :from self,\n   185\t                                                 :value value})]\n   186\t                              ;; an oversized reply is dropped, not\n   187\t                              ;; torn; the requester times out and\n   188\t                              ;; treats this peer as not holding the\n   189\t                              ;; value\n   190\t                              (when (<= (alength reply) max-datagram)\n   191\t                                (.send socket\n   192\t                                       (DatagramPacket. reply\n   193\t                                                        (alength reply)\n   194\t                                                        (.getAddress packet)\n   195\t                                                        (.getPort packet))))))\n   196\t                          ;; a hostile message (e.g. malformed :from)\n   197\t                          ;; is dropped, never fatal\n   198\t                          (catch Exception _ nil)))))))))\n   199\t       (.setDaemon true)\n   200\t       (.start))))\n   201\t\n   202\t\n   203\t;; =============================================================================\n   204\t;; Requests (the client half)\n   205\t;; =============================================================================\n   206\t\n   207\t#?(:cljd nil\n   208\t   :clj (defn- attempt!\n   209\t          [{:keys [^DatagramSocket socket ^ConcurrentHashMap pending\n   210\t                   ^AtomicLong rpc-counter peer]} to msg timeout-ms]\n   211\t          (let [rpc (.getAndIncrement rpc-counter)\n   212\t                p (promise)]\n   213\t            (.put pending rpc p)\n   214\t            (try (send-datagram! socket\n   215\t                                 (encode (assoc msg\n   216\t                                                :rpc rpc\n   217\t                                                :from peer))\n   218\t                                 (InetAddress/getByName (:host to))\n   219\t                                 (:port to))\n   220\t                 (deref p timeout-ms ::timeout)\n   221\t                 ;; an unresolvable host, a socket fault, or an oversized\n   222\t                 ;; payload is the same fact as silence: the peer is\n   223\t                 ;; unreachable, and the\n   224\t                 ;; IDhtNet contract wants nil, not a throw\n   225\t                 (catch Exception _ ::timeout)\n   226\t                 (finally (.remove pending rpc))))))\n   227\t\n   228\t\n   229\t#?(:cljd nil\n   230\t   :clj\n   231\t   (defn- request!\n   232\t     \"Request-response over fire-and-forget UDP: this client owns timeouts\n   233\t      and retries. Returns the reply value, or nil when the peer stays\n   234\t      unreachable.\"\n   235\t     [node to msg]\n   236\t     (let [{:keys [timeout-ms tries], :or {timeout-ms 500, tries 3}} (:opts\n   237\t                                                                       node)]\n   238\t       (loop [n tries]\n   239\t         (when (pos? n)\n   240\t           (let [res (attempt! node to msg timeout-ms)]\n   241\t             (if (= ::timeout res) (recur (dec n)) res)))))))\n   242\t\n   243\t\n   244\t;; =============================================================================\n   245\t;; The node\n   246\t;; =============================================================================\n   247\t\n   248\t#?(:cljd nil\n   249\t   :clj (defrecord UdpNode\n   250\t          [^DatagramSocket socket peer local table\n   251\t           ^ConcurrentHashMap pending ^AtomicLong rpc-counter\n   252\t           ^AtomicBoolean closed receiver opts]\n   253\t\n   254\t          dht/IDhtNet\n   255\t\n   256\t          (self-peer [_] peer)\n   257\t\n   258\t\n   259\t          (known-peers [_ target-id n] (kad/nearest @table target-id n))\n   260\t\n   261\t\n   262\t          (find-closer\n   263\t            [this to target-id]\n   264\t            (when-let [res (request! this\n   265\t                                     to\n   266\t                                     {:op :find-node, :target target-id})]\n   267\t              (let [found (remove #(= (:id %) (:id peer)) (:peers res))]\n   268\t                (doseq [q found] (swap! table kad/observe (:id peer) q))\n   269\t                found)))\n   270\t\n   271\t\n   272\t          (store-content!\n   273\t            [this to address payload]\n   274\t            (try (boolean (:ok (request! this\n   275\t                                         to\n   276\t                                         {:op :store-content,\n   277\t                                          :address address,\n   278\t                                          :v payload})))\n   279\t                 (catch Exception _ false)))\n   280\t\n   281\t\n   282\t          (fetch-content\n   283\t            [this to address]\n   284\t            (when-let [res (request! this\n   285\t                                     to\n   286\t                                     {:op :fetch-content, :address address})]\n   287\t              {:found? (boolean (:found res)), :value (:v res)}))\n   288\t\n   289\t\n   290\t          (close-net!\n   291\t            [_]\n   292\t            (when (.compareAndSet closed false true) (.close socket))\n   293\t            nil)))\n   294\t\n   295\t\n   296\t#?(:cljd nil\n   297\t   :clj\n   298\t   (defn create-node\n   299\t     \"Open a UDP Kademlia peer implementing dht/IDhtNet. opts:\n   300\t        :host        bind/advertised address (default \\\"127.0.0.1\\\")\n   301\t        :port        listen port (default 0 = ephemeral)\n   302\t        :local       the content handle this peer serves (default\n   303\t                     dao.jing.mem/create-content-mem)\n   304\t        :bootstrap   seq of {:host :port} of existing peers\n   305\t        :timeout-ms  per-attempt reply timeout (default 500)\n   306\t        :tries       attempts per request (default 3)\"\n   307\t     [{:keys [host port local bootstrap], :as opts}]\n   308\t     (let [host (or host \"127.0.0.1\")\n   309\t           socket (DatagramSocket. (int (or port 0)))\n   310\t           port (.getLocalPort socket)\n   311\t           local (or local (mem/create-content-mem))\n   312\t           peer {:id (dht/node-id host port), :host host, :port port}\n   313\t           table (atom {})\n   314\t           pending (ConcurrentHashMap.)\n   315\t           receiver (start-receiver! socket local table peer pending)\n   316\t           node (->UdpNode socket\n   317\t                           peer\n   318\t                           local\n   319\t                           table\n   320\t                           pending\n   321\t                           (AtomicLong. 0)\n   322\t                           (AtomicBoolean. false)\n   323\t                           receiver\n   324\t                           (select-keys opts [:timeout-ms :tries]))]\n   325\t       (doseq [b bootstrap]\n   326\t         ;; the ping reply's :from carries the bootstrap peer's id, which\n   327\t         ;; the receiver observes into the routing table\n   328\t         (request! node b {:op :ping}))\n   329\t       (when (seq bootstrap)\n   330\t         ;; iterative self-lookup populates the neighborhood\n   331\t         (dht/lookup node (:id peer)))\n   332\t       node)))\n   333\t\n   334\t\n   335\t#?(:cljd nil\n   336\t   :clj\n   337\t   (defn create-content-dht-udp\n   338\t     \"Convenience: open a UDP node and wrap it as a DHT content-store\n   339\t      handle. The node and the store share `local`, so this peer serves the\n   340\t      same bytes it reads.\"\n   341\t     [opts]\n   342\t     (let [local (or (:local opts) (mem/create-content-mem))\n   343\t           node (create-node (assoc opts :local local))]\n   344\t       (dht/create-content-dht {:net node, :local local}))))\n     1\t(ns dao.jing.dht.kad\n     2\t  \"Pure Kademlia routing primitives: XOR distance over hex node ids and a\n     3\t  k-bucket routing table. No IO lives here; the table is a plain value the\n     4\t  node threads through an atom. Ids are equal-length lowercase hex strings\n     5\t  (32 bytes, 64 chars) sharing one key space with content digests, so peers\n     6\t  and keys route uniformly.\")\n     7\t\n     8\t\n     9\t(def k\n    10\t  \"Bucket capacity and replication factor (Kademlia's k).\"\n    11\t  20)\n    12\t\n    13\t\n    14\t(def ^:private hex-val (zipmap \"0123456789abcdef\" (range 16)))\n    15\t\n    16\t\n    17\t(defn distance\n    18\t  \"XOR distance between two equal-length hex ids, as a vector of nibble\n    19\t  xors. Vectors compare lexicographically, so\n    20\t  (compare (distance a t) (distance b t)) orders a and b by closeness to t.\"\n    21\t  [id-a id-b]\n    22\t  (mapv (fn [ca cb] (bit-xor (hex-val ca) (hex-val cb))) id-a id-b))\n    23\t\n    24\t\n    25\t(defn- nibble-leading-zeros\n    26\t  [n]\n    27\t  (cond (>= n 8) 0\n    28\t        (>= n 4) 1\n    29\t        (>= n 2) 2\n    30\t        :else 3))\n    31\t\n    32\t\n    33\t(defn bucket-index\n    34\t  \"The k-bucket a peer belongs to: the number of leading bits its id shares\n    35\t  with self-id. nil when the ids are equal (a node never buckets itself).\"\n    36\t  [self-id peer-id]\n    37\t  (loop [nibbles (seq (distance self-id peer-id))\n    38\t         acc 0]\n    39\t    (when-let [[n & more] nibbles]\n    40\t      (if (zero? n) (recur more (+ acc 4)) (+ acc (nibble-leading-zeros n))))))\n    41\t\n    42\t\n    43\t(defn observe\n    44\t  \"Record contact with peer (a {:id :host :port} map), returning the new\n    45\t  table. The peer becomes most-recently-seen in its bucket; when a full\n    46\t  bucket overflows, the least-recently-seen peer is dropped. (Canonical\n    47\t  Kademlia pings the oldest and keeps it if it answers; that liveness\n    48\t  refinement can layer on later.)\"\n    49\t  [table self-id peer]\n    50\t  (if-let [idx (bucket-index self-id (:id peer))]\n    51\t    (update table\n    52\t            idx\n    53\t            (fn [bucket]\n    54\t              (let [bucket\n    55\t                    (into [] (remove #(= (:id %) (:id peer))) (or bucket []))\n    56\t                    bucket (if (>= (count bucket) k) (subvec bucket 1) bucket)]\n    57\t                (conj bucket peer))))\n    58\t    table))\n    59\t\n    60\t\n    61\t(defn nearest\n    62\t  \"The n known peers nearest target-id, nearest first.\"\n    63\t  [table target-id n]\n    64\t  (->> (vals table)\n    65\t       (mapcat identity)\n    66\t       (sort-by #(distance (:id %) target-id))\n    67\t       (take n)\n    68\t       vec))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/datom.world.md | sed -n '12,36p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.jing.md | sed -n '1,105p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md | sed -n '11,55p;310,390p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc | sed -n '1,52p;55,75p;151,195p;208,253p;280,355p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/jing/file.cljc | sed -n '1,38p;342,378p;405,425p'; rg -n 'memory-log|retention|durab|create-memory' src/cljc/dao/stream/memory_log.cljc docs/design/dao.stream.md; rg -n 'segment-address\\\\?|segment-hash|digest|algorithm' src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc | head -55; rg -n 'manifest|drain|index-store|publish' src/cljc/yin/repl/index.cljc | tail -45\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/datom.world.md | sed -n '12,36p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.jing.md | sed -n '1,105p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md | sed -n '11,55p;310,390p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc | sed -n '1,52p;55,75p;151,195p;208,253p;280,355p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/jing/file.cljc | sed -n '1,38p;342,378p;405,425p'; rg -n 'memory-log|retention|durab|create-memory' src/cljc/dao/stream/memory_log.cljc docs/design/dao.stream.md; rg -n 'segment-address\\\\?|segment-hash|digest|algorithm' src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc | head -55; rg -n 'manifest|drain|index-store|publish' src/cljc/yin/repl/index.cljc | tail -45\"","aggregated_output":"    12\tRestrictions are a feature.\n    13\t\n    14\t### Foundational Axioms\n    15\t\n    16\t1. **Everything is a Stream**: All IO and data flow through append-only streams.\n    17\t2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\n    18\t   At system scale, a \"system\" is an emergent property of interpreters of\n    19\t   pure data, not an object, service, or stored artifact. Find the\n    20\t   interpreters and the data they share to find the system.\n    21\t3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\n    22\t4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\n    23\t\n    24\t## Non-Negotiable Invariants\n    25\t\n    26\t- Do not introduce hidden global state.\n    27\t- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\n    28\t- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\n    29\t- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\n    30\t- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\n    31\t- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\n    32\t\n    33\t## Tuples and Datoms\n    34\t\n    35\tTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\n    36\tA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\n     1\t# DaoJing: The Content-Addressed Storage Observer\n     2\t\n     3\tNamed for 井 (jǐng), the well: shared storage everyone draws from, holding whatever is poured in and giving it back unchanged. Not 经, the canon, and not a reference to any scripture.\n     4\t\n     5\tStatus: implemented. The observer (`observer-state` / `observe-step!`) and the\n     6\tplain-data content-store handles described here are the current\n     7\t`src/cljc/dao/jing*.cljc` code, over the canonical CBOR codec of\n     8\t`dao.jing.cbor.md` and the byte-store handle contract. What remains open --\n     9\tthe ClojureDart constructor-metadata obligation, the intake transport's\n    10\tnarrower portable domain, decoded numeric carriers on JavaScript, durable\n    11\tobserver checkpoints, explicit materialization acknowledgement, the content\n    12\twrite path as an effect stream, garbage collection, and async hydration --\n    13\tis listed under *Open items and current limitations*.\n    14\t\n    15\t**Related documents:**\n    16\t\n    17\t- `docs/design/dao.space.md` — the tuple space built by interpreters above\n    18\t  storage\n    19\t- `docs/design/dao.space.index.md` — the owner-side covered-index publisher\n    20\t- `docs/design/dao.space.query.md` — the reader-side index consumer\n    21\t- `docs/design/dao.jing.dht.md` — the DHT distribution backend\n    22\t- `docs/design/dao.stream.md` — the append-only stream primitive\n    23\t- `docs/design/dao.data.btree.md` — the covered-index node format\n    24\t- `docs/design/datom.md` — the datom and tuple specification\n    25\t- `docs/design/adr/0001-dao-space-as-storage-boundary.md` — the storage-boundary\n    26\t  decision\n    27\t- `docs/datomic.md` — the Datomic storage architecture that informs the\n    28\t  separation of storage and interpretation\n    29\t\n    30\t## Definition\n    31\t\n    32\tA `dao.jing` observes an explicit pool of intake `dao.stream` values and\n    33\tmaterializes their elements into content-addressed key-value storage.\n    34\t\n    35\tFor every opaque payload `x` arriving through any stream in the pool, DaoJing\n    36\tperforms the same storage operation:\n    37\t\n    38\t```text\n    39\tbytes = canonical-encode(x)\n    40\tkey   = content-hash(bytes)\n    41\tKV[key] = x                 ; insert if absent\n    42\t```\n    43\t\n    44\tDaoJing knows how to:\n    45\t\n    46\t- observe the streams in its configured pool;\n    47\t- retain the operational cursor needed to continue reading each stream;\n    48\t- canonically encode an element;\n    49\t- derive its content address;\n    50\t- insert it idempotently into the materialization target; and\n    51\t- retrieve content by its address.\n    52\t\n    53\tDaoJing does not know whether an element is a datom, B-tree node, covered\n    54\tindex, manifest, program, image, or any other kind of value. Meaning belongs\n    55\tto the interpreter that produced or consumes the content.\n    56\t\n    57\tThis makes DaoJing suitable storage for **any point in Datom.world's moduli\n    58\tspace of database interpreters**. DaoSpace can store covered indexes and\n    59\tindexed snapshots in it; DaoField can store metric indexes; another interpreter\n    60\tcan store graph, document, columnar, model, or other materializations. All are\n    61\topaque values at this boundary.\n    62\t\n    63\tDaoJing is itself an interpretation of DaoStream, but only at the\n    64\trepresentation level: it interprets each emitted value as content to encode,\n    65\thash, and materialize in a key/value store. That operation assigns content\n    66\tidentity and retrieval semantics while remaining devoid of domain semantics.\n    67\tDaoJing is therefore shared storage beneath the database moduli space, not a\n    68\tsemantic database point within it.\n    69\t\n    70\tDaoJing maintains no membership registry, no mutable roots, no CAS records,\n    71\tand no delete operation. Its only object of discourse is the strict content\n    72\taddress `:segment/<algorithm>-<digest>`, and its only semantics are\n    73\tinsert-if-absent materialization plus content reads.\n    74\t\n    75\t## The intake pool\n    76\t\n    77\tThe pool contains the streams through which content is submitted for\n    78\tmaterialization. It is supplied explicitly when the observer state is\n    79\tconstructed. DaoJing performs no stream registration or discovery and\n    80\tmaintains no shared membership record.\n    81\t\n    82\tThe pool is an intake topology, not a semantic namespace. DaoJing does not use\n    83\tthe identity of the source stream when deriving an element's key, and it does\n    84\tnot attach source identity to the stored value:\n    85\t\n    86\t```text\n    87\tstream A emits x ─┐\n    88\t                  ├── hash(canonical-encode(x)) ──► the same KV entry\n    89\tstream B emits x ─┘\n    90\t```\n    91\t\n    92\tIdentical content therefore converges on the same address regardless of which\n    93\tpool member carried it. Pool order, source identity, and arrival order do not\n    94\tchange content identity.\n    95\t\n    96\tDaoJing must distinguish pool members operationally long enough to maintain a\n    97\tcursor for each one. That association is observer state only. It is not\n    98\twritten into the content address or materialized payload and has no semantic\n    99\tmeaning. In the implemented observer each member entry is plain data —\n   100\t`{:stream <v2 reader handle>, :cursor <opaque>, :status s}` — an operational\n   101\trecord, nothing more. `observer-state` rejects a member that lacks a cursor\n   102\tor a v2 reader before any operation. Besides successful observation, the only\n   103\tway a member's cursor changes is through `adopt-cursor`.\n   104\t\n   105\tIf a storage backend partitions content into physical buckets or shards, that\n    11\t## 1. Invariant, impossibilities, non-goals\n    12\t\n    13\tThe governing requirement, in the owner's words:\n    14\t\n    15\t> any implementation of dao.stream can be mechanically exposed via websocket\n    16\t> or udp and communicate p2p. client-server is just one stigmergic behavior\n    17\t> that interpreters implement. It should be able to traverse a NAT. A toy\n    18\t> example would be a string with a dao.stream wrapper that can be exposed on\n    19\t> a websocket on a remote interpreter.\n    20\t\n    21\t> There should be no concept of a server or client. its P2P but client/server\n    22\t> model can be implemented by convention. there is no priviledge server or\n    23\t> client.\n    24\t\n    25\t`dao.stream` is an abstraction boundary, not a network boundary. This\n    26\tdocument makes it a network boundary too, and keeps every network concept on\n    27\tits own side: nothing here adds an operation, an outcome or a key to the\n    28\tcontract beyond the five amendments listed in implementation plan section 2\n    29\t(OD-1, OD-2, OD-3, the `:dao.stream/refused` row, and the composed-handle\n    30\tsentence).\n    31\t\n    32\t**Impossibilities, stated plainly.**\n    33\t\n    34\t- Direct exchange needs one end that can receive an unsolicited packet, or a\n    35\t  coordinated simultaneous send. Where neither exists, a third peer with a\n    36\t  reachable address is required (section 4).\n    37\t- The unreachable cases are named by mapping and filtering behavior, not\n    38\t  product name: endpoint-dependent mapping and filtering on both sides (a\n    39\t  symmetric NAT; the strict paths of a carrier-grade NAT, which is address\n    40\t  sharing and not one behavior, since other carrier-grade paths hole-punch)\n    41\t  defeats punching, and relay is the workaround (section 4).\n    42\t- A browser cannot listen. It dials, or it is reached through a third peer.\n    43\t- Every peer needs an outbound path to a reachable WebSocket peer or relay.\n    44\t  If UDP is blocked and no such path exists, communication is impossible.\n    45\t- Everything a channel carries is readable by every peer that carries or\n    46\t  receives it until encryption is composed, and the encryption this version\n    47\t  specifies is value-level only: a relay still sees every envelope,\n    48\t  identities, ops, cursors, ids, sizes and timing included (section 7).\n    49\t- UDP messages are bounded by a composed maximum (section 3.2).\n    50\t- A reclaimed lease means the resource is gone, not disconnected (section 6).\n    51\t- A false lapse under partition is possible (`dao.lease.md`, Limits).\n    52\t\n    53\t**Non-goals.** Authentication, authorization and key distribution (section 7\n    54\tnames the seam; `dao.shibi.md` owns the rest); channel-level encryption\n    55\t(deferred, section 7); WebRTC data channels; browser inbound; readiness\n   310\tretention at the answering peer: the cursor is the sequence number for\n   311\treads, and `gap` remains the source's declaration, never a channel artifact.\n   312\t\n   313\t## 3. Channels\n   314\t\n   315\tA channel declares a portable value domain, a frame budget, and whether it\n   316\tis ordered and reliable; the link reads the last as composition data for\n   317\t`:dao.stream.remote/resend-after`, and nothing else in section 2 changes\n   318\tper channel.\n   319\t\n   320\t### 3.1 WebSocket\n   321\t\n   322\t`dao.stream.ws` as specified in `dao.stream.ws.md` is the socket layer, and\n   323\ta channel is one of its attachments plus a projection. **Reused**: the\n   324\tattachment model and identity; the adapter that deposits every event as\n   325\t`{:ws/attachment id :ws/event e :ws/value v}` on the medium the composition\n   326\twired; the writer handle, whose `append!` frames each value as one\n   327\t`{:ws/frame :ws/value :ws/value v}` message in the negotiated codec profile\n   328\t(Transit-JSON text or the CBOR binary profile); the bounded acceptance\n   329\thandoff with slot and acknowledgement; the resolution and lifecycle\n   330\tvocabulary. Ordered and reliable; frame budget unbounded.\n   331\t\n   332\t**New, the projection.** The mirror and the link speak raw request and\n   333\tanswer maps, so each channel end composes one step, `ws-project`, between\n   334\tthe ws deposit medium and the channel. The channel reader is a ring buffer\n   335\tthe composition wires; the channel writer is the ws handle itself.\n   336\t`ws-project` holds the reading cursor on the attachment's traffic medium,\n   337\tkeeps the events whose `:ws/attachment` names this channel, appends the\n   338\t`:ws/value` of each `:ws/payload` onto the ring buffer, and drops\n   339\t`:ws/error` diagnostics. A terminal lifecycle event for the attachment\n   340\t(`:ws/closed`, `:ws/ended`) or a failure resolution (`:ws/not-found`,\n   341\t`:ws/transport-error`) makes `ws-project` close the ring buffer: channel\n   342\tloss is then the link's `end` observation (2.4). `ws-project` is a step the\n   343\tcomposition drives at its own cadence, as it drives `endpoint-step`.\n   344\t\n   345\tDirection is establishment, not authority. A WebSocket has a dialer and an\n   346\tacceptor because TCP does. Once established the channel is symmetric and\n   347\tnothing in this document remembers who dialed. Hosts: clj, cljs on Node and\n   348\tcljd may dial or accept (`src/clj/dao/stream/ws/jvm.clj`,\n   349\t`src/cljs/dao/stream/ws/node.cljs`, `src/cljd/dao/stream/ws/dart.cljd`);\n   350\tcljs in a browser may only dial (`src/cljs/dao/stream/ws/browser.cljs`).\n   351\t\n   352\t### 3.2 UDP\n   353\t\n   354\t`dao.stream.udp` is a new channel with the WebSocket deposit model: a socket\n   355\tretains nothing, so it has no reader surface, and its adapter deposits every\n   356\tdatagram as an event carrying the datagram's source address and its decoded\n   357\tvalue onto the medium the composition wired.\n   358\t\n   359\t- Descriptor: `{:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/udp :dao.stream/identity\n   360\t  <channel identity> :dao.stream.udp/host h :dao.stream.udp/port p}`.\n   361\t  `attach!` binds or reuses a local socket and returns a handle whose writer\n   362\t  path is datagrams to that address.\n   363\t- One CBOR value per datagram, canonical profile. The budget covers the\n   364\t  whole encoded datagram, fragment envelope included: at most 1200 bytes,\n   365\t  the figure `dao.jing.dht.node` already uses to clear common path MTUs.\n   366\t- **Fragmentation is channel-internal.** An encoded message over budget is\n   367\t  split into `k` datagrams, each\n   368\t  `{:dao.stream.remote/id n :dao.stream.udp/part i :dao.stream.udp/parts k\n   369\t  :dao.stream.udp/bytes b}`, where `n` is the carried message's own request\n   370\t  id. The receiver reassembles by `[channel attachment identity,\n   371\t  source-address, direction, id]`, because ids are unique only per channel\n   372\t  reader and one socket may serve several attachments, and delivers only a\n   373\t  complete message. Loss of any part is loss of the whole message,\n   374\t  recovered by the link's resend rule. Reassembly is bounded by two\n   375\t  composition data, `:dao.stream.udp/max-message-bytes` (default 64 KiB)\n   376\t  and `:dao.stream.udp/max-partial-messages`, evicting the oldest partial\n   377\t  message; no clock. Neither the mirror step nor the link sees a fragment.\n   378\t- Replies go to the datagram's source address, never to an address claimed\n   379\t  in a payload.\n   380\t- A send may name an explicit destination from an already-bound socket. Hole\n   381\t  punching depends on it (section 4).\n   382\t- Unordered and best-effort. Attachment identity is the remote host and port\n   383\t  as the socket saw them; it is the reflexive address a meeting peer\n   384\t  republishes.\n   385\t\n   386\tHosts: clj (`DatagramSocket`), cljs on Node (`dgram`), cljd\n   387\t(`RawDatagramSocket`). Browsers have no UDP.\n   388\t\n   389\t### 3.3 Pair\n   390\t\n     1\t(ns dao.stream.udp\n     2\t  \"The UDP channel of dao.stream.remote.md (3.2): the WebSocket deposit\n     3\t   model over a socket that retains nothing.\n     4\t\n     5\t   `make-port` composes the local socket's protocol state, host-free: a\n     6\t   host adapter feeds every received datagram to `receive!` with its\n     7\t   raw bytes and the source address the socket saw. One CBOR value,\n     8\t   canonical profile, rides one datagram, at most `max-datagram` bytes,\n     9\t   fragment envelope included -- the figure `dao.jing.dht.node` already\n    10\t   uses to clear common path MTUs. An encoded message over that budget\n    11\t   is split into fragment envelopes, channel-internal: neither the\n    12\t   mirror step nor the link (dao.stream.remote) ever sees one, only the\n    13\t   complete decoded message `receive!` reassembles and deposits.\n    14\t\n    15\t   Reassembly key, per 3.2: [attachment identity, source address,\n    16\t   direction, id]. For UDP the attachment identity a socket serves IS\n    17\t   the remote host:port it saw, so this port's own reassembly key\n    18\t   collapses the first two components into one address string; a\n    19\t   fragment's own carried id is unique only per asking side, so the\n    20\t   direction distinguishes a fragment split from an outgoing request\n    21\t   from one split from its answer, both landing at the same address\n    22\t   with the same id, when this side is both asker and mirror on one\n    23\t   channel. Bounded by two composition data, no clock:\n    24\t   `:dao.stream.udp/max-message-bytes` (default 64 KiB) refuses an\n    25\t   oversize send and bounds a reassembly's accumulated chunk bytes;\n    26\t   and `:dao.stream.udp/max-partial-messages`, evicting the oldest\n    27\t   partial message.\n    28\t\n    29\t   The deposit adapter shape: every reassembled datagram value is\n    30\t   deposited as one event, `{:dao.stream.udp/attachment id\n    31\t   :dao.stream.udp/source {:dao.stream.udp/host h\n    32\t   :dao.stream.udp/port p} :dao.stream.udp/value v}`, onto the shared\n    33\t   traffic medium the composition wires -- one socket may serve several\n    34\t   attachments, one per remote address, so this medium is shared the\n    35\t   way a WebSocket connection's own traffic medium is not. `projection`\n    36\t   and `step!` are this channel's `ws-project`: driven per attachment\n    37\t   at the composition's cadence, they keep the events naming their own\n    38\t   attachment and append each value onto the channel ring that\n    39\t   attachment's reflections read.\n    40\t\n    41\t   `make-attacher` binds or reuses `:port` and answers a writer handle\n    42\t   whose `append!` sends toward the descriptor's address, fragmenting\n    43\t   and refusing oversize per the above; `send-to!` is the same write\n    44\t   path to an explicit destination from an already-bound port, for hole\n    45\t   punching (section 4), outside any attach!'d descriptor. Replies\n    46\t   always go to a datagram's own source address, never a payload's\n    47\t   claim, because `receive!` is the only place a source address is\n    48\t   read, and every deposited event carries the address the socket\n    49\t   itself saw it arrive from.\"\n    50\t  (:require [dao.stream :as stream]\n    51\t            [dao.stream.cbor :as cbor])\n    52\t  #?(:cljd (:import [\"dart:typed_data\" Uint8List])))\n    55\t(def transport-type :dao.stream/udp)\n    56\t\n    57\t\n    58\t(def max-datagram\n    59\t  \"The whole encoded datagram's budget, fragment envelope included: the\n    60\t   1200-byte figure dao.jing.dht.node already uses to clear common path\n    61\t   MTUs (docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md 3.2).\"\n    62\t  1200)\n    63\t\n    64\t\n    65\t(def default-max-message-bytes\n    66\t  \"64 KiB, the default reassembly maximum (3.2).\"\n    67\t  (* 64 1024))\n    68\t\n    69\t\n    70\t(def default-max-partial-messages\n    71\t  \"No default is named in 3.2; this is this implementation's own bound\n    72\t   on outstanding partial messages, chosen only to keep reassembly\n    73\t   memory finite without a clock.\"\n    74\t  64)\n    75\t\n   151\t(defn- fragment-envelope\n   152\t  [id part parts direction chunk]\n   153\t  {:dao.stream.remote/id id\n   154\t   :dao.stream.udp/part part\n   155\t   :dao.stream.udp/parts parts\n   156\t   :dao.stream.udp/direction direction\n   157\t   :dao.stream.udp/bytes chunk})\n   158\t\n   159\t\n   160\t(defn- fits?\n   161\t  [id part parts direction chunk-len]\n   162\t  (<= (blen (cbor/encode\n   163\t              (fragment-envelope id part parts direction\n   164\t                                 (balloc chunk-len))))\n   165\t      max-datagram))\n   166\t\n   167\t\n   168\t(defn- chunk-size\n   169\t  \"The largest chunk length whose fragment envelope, at the highest\n   170\t   part index (the widest of the small integers), still fits the\n   171\t   datagram budget; backs off from an optimistic start.\"\n   172\t  [id parts direction]\n   173\t  (loop [size (- max-datagram 16)]\n   174\t    (when (pos? size)\n   175\t      (if (fits? id (dec parts) parts direction size)\n   176\t        size\n   177\t        (recur (dec size))))))\n   178\t\n   179\t\n   180\t(defn- fragments\n   181\t  \"Split encoded `payload` for message `id`, direction `direction`,\n   182\t   into fragment envelopes that each fit the datagram budget.\"\n   183\t  [payload id direction]\n   184\t  (let [total (blen payload)\n   185\t        parts (max 1 (ceil-div total (- max-datagram 16)))\n   186\t        size (or (chunk-size id parts direction)\n   187\t                 (throw (ex-info\n   188\t                          \"dao.stream.udp: no chunk size fits the budget\"\n   189\t                          {:id id :parts parts})))\n   190\t        parts (max parts (ceil-div total size))\n   191\t        size (or (chunk-size id parts direction) size)]\n   192\t    (for [i (range parts)\n   193\t          :let [start (* i size)\n   194\t                end (min total (+ start size))]]\n   195\t      (fragment-envelope id i parts direction (bslice payload start end)))))\n   208\t   (`dao.stream/writer?`). `:max-message-bytes` and\n   209\t   `:max-partial-messages` are the two reassembly bounds (defaults\n   210\t   above).\"\n   211\t  [{:keys [send! traffic max-message-bytes max-partial-messages]}]\n   212\t  (when-not (and (fn? send!) (stream/writer? traffic))\n   213\t    (throw (ex-info \"invalid dao.stream.udp port composition\"\n   214\t                    {:send! send! :traffic traffic})))\n   215\t  (atom {:send! send!\n   216\t         :traffic traffic\n   217\t         :max-message-bytes (or max-message-bytes\n   218\t                                default-max-message-bytes)\n   219\t         :max-partial-messages (or max-partial-messages\n   220\t                                   default-max-partial-messages)\n   221\t         :partial {}\n   222\t         :partial-order []}))\n   223\t\n   224\t\n   225\t(defn- send-datagram!\n   226\t  [port host dest-port bytes]\n   227\t  ((:send! @port) host dest-port bytes))\n   228\t\n   229\t\n   230\t(defn send-value!\n   231\t  \"Encode and send `v` toward `host`:`dest-port` from `port`'s socket:\n   232\t   a single datagram when it fits the budget, fragments when it does\n   233\t   not, transport-error naming oversize when the encoded message\n   234\t   exceeds :dao.stream.udp/max-message-bytes -- never sent, torn or\n   235\t   partially sent.\"\n   236\t  [port host dest-port v]\n   237\t  (let [payload (cbor/encode v)\n   238\t        n (blen payload)]\n   239\t    (cond\n   240\t      (> n (:max-message-bytes @port))\n   241\t      {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/transport-error\n   242\t       :dao.stream.remote/reason :dao.stream.remote/oversize}\n   243\t\n   244\t      (<= n max-datagram)\n   245\t      (do (send-datagram! port host dest-port payload)\n   246\t          {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok})\n   247\t\n   248\t      :else\n   249\t      (let [id (:dao.stream.remote/id v)\n   250\t            direction (direction-of v)]\n   251\t        (doseq [frag (fragments payload id direction)]\n   252\t          (send-datagram! port host dest-port (cbor/encode frag)))\n   253\t        {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok}))))\n   280\t(defn- well-formed-fragment?\n   281\t  \"A fragment envelope's own wire fields, checked before any reassembly\n   282\t   state can change: :part and :parts are integers with 0 <= :part <\n   283\t   :parts, 1 <= :parts, :direction one of the two the write side\n   284\t   mints, and :bytes a host byte payload. A datagram failing this is\n   285\t   best-effort wire input, dropped below the mirror and the link like\n   286\t   any other malformed one -- nothing here may throw during\n   287\t   reassembly, whatever a peer put on the wire.\"\n   288\t  [v]\n   289\t  (let [{:dao.stream.udp/keys [part parts direction bytes]} v]\n   290\t    (and (integer? part)\n   291\t         (integer? parts)\n   292\t         (pos? parts)\n   293\t         (<= 0 part)\n   294\t         (< part parts)\n   295\t         (#{:dao.stream.udp/request :dao.stream.udp/answer} direction)\n   296\t         (cbor/byte-payload? bytes))))\n   297\t\n   298\t\n   299\t(defn- forget-partial!\n   300\t  \"Drop the partial message keyed `k` from the port's reassembly\n   301\t   state, its order entry with it.\"\n   302\t  [port k]\n   303\t  (swap! port (fn [s]\n   304\t                (-> s\n   305\t                    (update :partial dissoc k)\n   306\t                    (update :partial-order\n   307\t                            (fn [order] (vec (remove #{k} order))))))))\n   308\t\n   309\t\n   310\t(defn- evict-oldest!\n   311\t  [port]\n   312\t  (when-some [k (first (:partial-order @port))]\n   313\t    (forget-partial! port k)))\n   314\t\n   315\t\n   316\t(defn- absorb-fragment!\n   317\t  \"Fold one well-formed fragment envelope into the port's reassembly\n   318\t   state, keyed by [attachment direction id]; returns the complete\n   319\t   encoded payload once every part has arrived, else nil. The\n   320\t   max-message-bytes bound is the accumulated chunk bytes themselves,\n   321\t   as they arrive: a message whose parts add up past the bound is\n   322\t   dropped the moment they say so, and a completed payload is\n   323\t   delivered only if its own actual length is within the bound -- no\n   324\t   declared-parts arithmetic stands in for the real bytes, so a valid\n   325\t   message near the limit delivers. A duplicate part is ignored, and\n   326\t   a fragment disagreeing with the parts count a partial already\n   327\t   holds is dropped, so the state held is never torn.\"\n   328\t  [port attachment frag]\n   329\t  (let [{:dao.stream.remote/keys [id]\n   330\t         :dao.stream.udp/keys [part parts direction bytes]} frag\n   331\t        k [attachment direction id]\n   332\t        bound (:max-message-bytes @port)]\n   333\t    (when-not (contains? (:partial @port) k)\n   334\t      (swap! port (fn [s]\n   335\t                    (-> s\n   336\t                        (assoc-in [:partial k]\n   337\t                                  {:parts {} :parts-count parts\n   338\t                                   :bytes-total 0})\n   339\t                        (update :partial-order conj k))))\n   340\t      (when (> (count (:partial @port))\n   341\t               (:max-partial-messages @port))\n   342\t        (evict-oldest! port)))\n   343\t    (when-some [held (get (:partial @port) k)]\n   344\t      (when (and (= parts (:parts-count held))\n   345\t                 (not (contains? (:parts held) part)))\n   346\t        (let [total (+ (:bytes-total held) (blen bytes))]\n   347\t          (if (> total bound)\n   348\t            ;; even completed, this message is over the bound: its\n   349\t            ;; partial state goes now, it can never deliver\n   350\t            (forget-partial! port k)\n   351\t            (do (swap! port (fn [s]\n   352\t                              (-> s\n   353\t                                  (assoc-in [:partial k :parts part] bytes)\n   354\t                                  (assoc-in [:partial k :bytes-total] total))))\n   355\t                (let [held (get (:partial @port) k)]\n     1\t(ns dao.jing.file\n     2\t  \"Content-addressed segment store backed by a private framed append-only\n     3\t   file (docs/design/dao.jing.md; docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md, Memory and\n     4\t   files; dao.jing.implementation-plan.md, Decision 2 and invariants\n     5\t   F1-F6).\n     6\t\n     7\t   Framing: each record is one frame of [4-byte big-endian signed length]\n     8\t   [CBOR array of two byte strings: the raw digest the address carries,\n     9\t   then the exact canonical payload bytes]. The length prefix is the\n    10\t   mechanism F3 needs -- it is what makes an incomplete tail detectable: a\n    11\t   tail shorter than the prefix, a negative length, or a length reaching\n    12\t   past end of file is torn. The two-element array is this backend's own\n    13\t   frame, written and parsed with Jing's shared codec (the one exception\n    14\t   docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md names, because this is the only backend\n    15\t   that re-ingests its own output across process death). The algorithm is\n    16\t   not carried: at replay the payload is hashed under each registered\n    17\t   algorithm and the one whose digest the frame carries names the address.\n    18\t   The outer frame never contributes to a content hash; the payload bytes\n    19\t   are stored and served exactly as written, never replaced by a decoded\n    20\t   and re-encoded value.\n    21\t\n    22\t   Opening validates before it mutates: every complete frame must parse,\n    23\t   carry a digest of its payload, and hold a canonical payload, and a\n    24\t   nonempty file must yield at least one such frame, or the open fails\n    25\t   with the file untouched. Only then is an incomplete tail truncated. No\n    26\t   magic, no header: a file that is not a content log fails loudly, and\n    27\t   an interrupted first write (never acknowledged) is recreated, not\n    28\t   recovered.\n    29\t\n    30\t   The durable log is not a stream (Decision 2): one reader -- this backend,\n    31\t   once, at open -- and one writer -- its own put. No cursor is handed out,\n    32\t   no descriptor projected, nothing attaches, and no dao.stream namespace is\n    33\t   required.\n    34\t\n    35\t   The handle is a plain-data byte store (D1): {:put-bytes-fn f\n    36\t   :get-bytes-fn g :close-fn c} and nothing else. Bytes are copied on the\n    37\t   way in and on the way out, so no caller can change a stored snapshot.\n    38\t   records (F5) is the test's view of a path: every decoded frame in order,\n   342\t(defn- make-put\n   343\t  [state f lock]\n   344\t  (fn content-put!\n   345\t    [address bs]\n   346\t    ;; copy first: the caller owns bs and may change it at any moment, so\n   347\t    ;; validation, framing and every comparison see only the snapshot\n   348\t    (let [snapshot (cbor/copy-bytes bs)]\n   349\t      (when-not (jing/segment-address? address)\n   350\t        (throw (ex-info \"Not a segment content address\" {:address address})))\n   351\t      (when-not (jing/segment-bytes-match? address snapshot)\n   352\t        (throw (ex-info \"Segment address does not match the bytes\"\n   353\t                        {:address address})))\n   354\t      (let [frame (frame-bytes address snapshot)]\n   355\t        (with-lock\n   356\t          lock\n   357\t          (fn []\n   358\t            (let [{:keys [closed? content]} @state]\n   359\t              (when closed?\n   360\t                (throw (ex-info \"Content file is closed\" {:address address})))\n   361\t              (if (contains? content address)\n   362\t                (if (cbor/bytes= (clojure.core/get content address) snapshot)\n   363\t                  :present\n   364\t                  (throw\n   365\t                    (ex-info\n   366\t                      (str \"Collision: address already holds a \"\n   367\t                           \"different payload\")\n   368\t                      {:address address})))\n   369\t                (do\n   370\t                  (append-frame! f frame)\n   371\t                  (sync-file! f)\n   372\t                  (swap! state assoc-in [:content address] snapshot)\n   373\t                  :inserted)))))))))\n   374\t\n   375\t\n   376\t(defn- make-get\n   377\t  [state]\n   378\t  (fn content-get\n   405\t(defn create-content-file\n   406\t  \"Open or create a content file at path: validate every complete frame,\n   407\t   truncate any incomplete tail (F3), replay every record into an\n   408\t   in-memory map of address to payload bytes (F2, F4), and return the\n   409\t   plain-data byte-store handle {:put-bytes-fn :get-bytes-fn :close-fn}\n   410\t   (D1). A\n   411\t   frame that is not a canonical [digest payload] record, whose digest is\n   412\t   not its payload's under a registered algorithm, or whose payload is not\n   413\t   canonical fails the open, loudly and without mutation (F4).\"\n   414\t  [path]\n   415\t  (let [f (open-file! path)]\n   416\t    (try (let [recovered (replay-records (recover! f))\n   417\t               state (atom {:closed? false, :content recovered})\n   418\t               lock #?(:clj (Object.)\n   419\t                       :cljs (js-obj)\n   420\t         