{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-09-30 06:41:01 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-30 13:41:01 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\n\n## A. Critique of slices 1–5\n\n1. **Fragmentation: necessary, but the proposed slice crosses a protocol boundary.** The current DHT refuses requests above 1200 bytes and drops oversized replies, so large index nodes cannot reliably leave the local store ([node.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/dht/node.cljc:74), [node.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/dht/node.cljc:186)). The existing `dao.stream.udp` framing is defined for remote-stream request IDs and directions, with a 64 KiB default message limit ([udp.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc:151), [udp.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc:230)). **I disagree** that the DHT node can simply reuse that private envelope or that moving its Transit-JSON wire to CBOR is required. Either run DHT messages over a specified stream channel, or extract a transport-neutral fragmenter with its own message identity and bounds. Preserve the current wire during an independent framing change unless a versioned migration is justified. Whole-content hash checking already protects accepted bytes ([dht.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc:167)); per-fragment hashes are optional, but reassembly limits, source scoping, expiry or eviction, and retries are required.\n\n2. **Replication: the blocking put is real; the proposed acknowledgement is unsound.** A put waits for peer attempts after its local write ([dht.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc:203)); each request can take three 500 ms attempts ([node.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/dht/node.cljc:231)). **I disagree** with “durably stored locally and queued” when the queue is `dao.stream.memory-log`: that log explicitly lasts only for the process ([memory_log.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/memory_log.cljc:1)). Choose one honest contract before coding: local durability with *volatile, best-effort* replication, or local durability plus a separately durable replication intent recorded before acknowledgement. An empty bootstrap must retain pending intent for later peer discovery under the latter contract; silently dropping it contradicts “queued.” Define bounded backlog, retry, shutdown, and recovery behavior. The local backend must be `dao.jing.file` for a durable claim; the DHT constructor currently accepts memory as local storage ([dht.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc:285)).\n\n3. **Non-JVM support: transport choice precedes host ports.** The author’s separate CLJD and Node UDP implementations are possible, but **I disagree** that they are the preferred architectural slice. The owner’s invariant makes any stream exposable through WebSocket or UDP, with NAT traversal and no privileged server role ([dao.stream.remote.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:13)). A DHT `IDhtNet` adapter over symmetric `dao.stream.remote` channels fits that boundary better than three independent socket protocol stacks. Reuse the existing UDP channel where applicable; use WebSocket or relay paths where raw UDP is unavailable. A browser cannot listen or use raw UDP ([dao.stream.remote.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:32)). Preserve `IDhtNet` as a useful seam ([dht.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc:53)), but specify peer identity, reply correlation, deadlines, and channel loss through stream events, consistent with the six invariants ([datom.world.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/datom.world.md:24)).\n\n4. **Hardening: correct concern, incomplete mechanism.** The receiver learns claimed `:from` peers before validating returnability and processes requests on its receiving thread ([node.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/dht/node.cljc:144)). A cookie must bind the observed source address and port, a short validity period, and a server-held secret; issue it in a small challenge reply and require it before expensive lookup responses, content reads, or store processing. Check reply size against request size until validation; cap bytes, work, fragments, pending RPCs, and storage per source and globally. A per-peer token bucket keyed only by claimed `:from` is spoofable. Also match replies to the expected source, not merely the RPC number ([node.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/dht/node.cljc:176)). This is an architectural threat-model decision followed by implementation work.\n\n5. **REPL integration: premature as slice 5.** The DHT has no roots by design ([dao.jing.dht.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.jing.dht.md:9)), while the durable REPL design needs a validated published HEAD and recovery path ([durable-store findings](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790709703000-architect-repl-durable-index-store-startup.gpt-6-sol.findings.md:15)). **I disagree** that a `:dht` startup spec and content fetch alone produce a usable cross-process index. A reader needs a trusted current manifest address, bootstrap reachability, and a complete fetchable index. The proposed plan also omits a slice for root discovery, availability repair, and storage limits. The existing lookup can strand live peers behind dead closest peers ([dht.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc:107)); best-effort replication plus unbounded storage does not establish persistent network availability ([dao.jing.dht.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.jing.dht.md:169)).\n\nThe DHT document’s “sha256 only” statement is stale: the implemented store accepts registered segment algorithms ([dao.jing.dht.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.jing.dht.md:17), [dht.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc:299)); Jing’s registry includes BLAKE3 and SHA-256, with BLAKE3 the default ([jing.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:207)).\n\n## B. Owner-policy recommendations\n\n1. **Keep `mem` as the default; require explicit `dht:`.** That preserves the agreed startup contract and prevents a change in latency, durability, network exposure, and failure modes merely by upgrading the REPL ([durable-store findings](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790709703000-architect-repl-durable-index-store-startup.gpt-6-sol.findings.md:7)). A `dht:` choice must name a durable local directory if it promises persistence.\n\n2. **Keep networking off until explicitly configured.** Empty bootstrap alone is insufficient: the current UDP constructor still binds a socket and starts a receiver ([node.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/dht/node.cljc:307)). Define local-only as *no listener, outbound dial, or replication worker*; enabling networking requires an explicit peer or meeting configuration. Once enabled, bootstrap addresses are ordinary peers, never authorities. NAT failure remains an observable connectivity state, not silent success ([dao.stream.remote.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:32)).\n\n3. **Do not publish sensitive REPL content to an open DHT before authorization exists.** “Sensitive” means anything the REPL intake can contain that the user has not explicitly marked public: source text, literals and secrets, AST or code rows, execution state, metadata, and index nodes or manifests that reveal those facts. Jing is deliberately payload-agnostic, so it cannot infer sensitivity ([dao.jing.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.jing.md:30)). Enforce the policy at REPL composition: reject network-enabled `dht:` publication by default; allow only an explicit public-data opt-in for that index, with a clear statement that peers can retain and redistribute every published segment. A warning alone does not enforce it. Encryption without key and access policy would still expose addresses and traffic metadata ([dao.stream.remote.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:45)).\n\n## C. Decisions before implementation\n\nDecide now: the acknowledgement and outbox durability contract; whether DHT traffic uses `dao.stream.remote` or a standalone versioned wire; maximum segment and reassembly size; the security boundary and public opt-in; what “local-only” opens; root-fact authority and conflict rule; and the availability promise (including what a peer acknowledgement proves). These shape interfaces and acceptance tests.\n\nPer slice, choose concrete codec, fragment sizing, retry timings, token-bucket values, host adapters, and CLI syntax. Root discovery can remain outside the DHT without a privileged node: a writer appends signed `{writer-id, sequence, previous-root, manifest-address}` facts to an explicitly configured durable `dao.stream`; peers expose or relay that stream via `dao.stream.remote`. A reader obtains a descriptor through configured peers or a meeting stream, verifies the trusted writer key and monotonic chain, then fetches the manifest by address. The stream’s location is bootstrap information; competing writers require an explicit application policy, not DHT consensus.\n\n## D. Recommended slice plan\n\n| Order | Slice and acceptance criterion |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | **Contract and local mode.** Specify the decisions in C, correct the stale hash documentation, and expose DHT local-only with a file backend. Restart retains acknowledged content; no socket opens without explicit network configuration. |\n| 2 | **Transport and large messages.** Implement the selected stream-channel `IDhtNet` path and bounded fragmentation. Cross-host put/fetch of manifest and index-node-sized payloads succeeds under loss and reordering; oversize input fails explicitly; hash mismatch never enters the local store. |\n| 3 | **Asynchronous replication.** Implement the chosen durable or explicitly volatile intent contract. Put returns after its stated local guarantee; empty-bootstrap intents behave as specified; restart, retries, backpressure, and close preserve that contract. |\n| 4 | **Security and availability.** Add returnability, source-bound reply validation, resource limits, dead-peer replacement, and repair or republishing policy. Spoofed and oversized traffic stays within stated bounds; a live next-nearest peer can supply content when nearer peers fail. |\n| 5 | **Root-fact stream.** Publish and read signed monotonic root facts over a configured remote stream. A fresh reader given only trust key and bootstrap descriptor finds the latest accepted manifest; stale, forged, and conflicting facts have specified outcomes. |\n| 6 | **`yin.repl` integration.** After the in-flight durable-store work lands, add explicit `dht:` startup selection, public-data gate, recovery, and read-only cross-process query. A second process discovers the root, fetches and verifies the full covered index, and queries prior code; offline restart uses the local durable copy. |\n\nSlices 2 and 3 can proceed concurrently after slice 1 if their transport and intent interfaces are fixed. Slice 4 can develop alongside them against those interfaces; slice 5 can proceed independently of REPL work. **No slice before 6 touches `yin/repl/*`.** The current round publishes blobs, drains intake, and reads its manifest back ([index.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/index.cljc:232)); slice 6 must preserve that local publication guarantee while reporting network publication as a separate, explicit state."}}
